---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro'
import PhotoPlaceholder from '../../components/PhotoPlaceholder.astro'

const title = 'Durham County Golf Union — Pin High Media'
const description = 'How Pin High rebuilt the Durham County Golf Union website from the ground up — a custom GolfGenius connector, live competition data, and Google Spreadsheets-driven dynamic content.'

const products = ['GolfGenius Connector', 'Integrated Website']
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>

  <section class="ph-page-head">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <div class="ph-back"><a href="/case-studies">← All case studies</a></div>
      <h1 class="ph-page-h1">Durham County Golf Union</h1>
      <p class="ph-page-lede">
        A county website rebuilt from the ground up.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="ph-container">
      <div class="ph-case-detail-art">
        <PhotoPlaceholder kind="aerial" ratio="21/9" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-case-detail-body">
    <div class="ph-container ph-case-detail-grid">
      <div>
        <p>
          Durham County had invested in a WordPress site — customised by an external company who also built a plugin connecting directly to GolfGenius, pulling in live competition results and upcoming events. For most county organisations, that single integration is reason enough never to move.
        </p>
        <p>
          The problem was the company had stopped updating the site. Years passed. The world moved on. The site didn't.
        </p>
        <p>
          I looked at the GolfGenius plugin and saw a straightforward API connection. So I built a better version of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The GolfGenius connector I built does everything the original plugin did — and more. Live scoring for county competitions displays directly on the homepage. Upcoming events pull through automatically. No manual updates, no copy and pasting results.
        </p>
        <p>
          The bigger problem with the old site was WordPress data tables — cumbersome to manage, slow to update, and a constant source of friction for the team. We got rid of them entirely.
        </p>
        <p>
          The new site is built on HTML and PHP. Dynamic content throughout connects directly to Google Spreadsheets — the county team update a row in a spreadsheet and the website reflects it immediately. No WordPress admin, no data tables, no technical knowledge needed. Just a spreadsheet.
        </p>
        <p>
          The result is a fast, modern website that the county can actually maintain themselves — with live competition data, dynamic content, and none of the WordPress baggage.
        </p>

        <ul class="ph-case-detail-list">
          {products.map((p) => <li>{p}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-closing">
    <div class="ph-closing-inner">
      <h2 class="ph-closing-h">Software that understands golf.</h2>
      <p class="ph-closing-p">Tell us what your club, county, or tour is wrestling with — we probably know the problem already.</p>
      <div class="ph-hero-ctas ph-closing-ctas">
        <a href="/contact" class="ph-btn ph-btn-primary">
          Get in touch
          <svg viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M2 6h8m-3-3 3 3-3 3"/></svg>
        </a>
        <a href="/case-studies" class="ph-btn ph-btn-ghost">
          All case studies
          <svg viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M2 6h8m-3-3 3 3-3 3"/></svg>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Layout>
